            <div xml:id="d540">

                <pb facs="0874" n="754" xml:id="pg754"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.34/183: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Schurman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-07-11T18:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">July 11, 1922—6
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received July 11—4:27
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>302. Your 161, June 29, 5 p.m. Chinese text of note signifies that in view of
                    Ministry of Navy the entire project should naturally be canceled and the case
                    terminated. Clause under discussion does not specify contract but uses more
                    comprehensive term, connoting the business in its entirety, namely, contract and
                    negotiations arising therefrom.</p>
                <p>The reason adduced by Ministry of Navy for arriving at this decision is the
                    inability of Bethlehem Corporation and the American Government to undertake to
                    carry out the contract.</p>
                <p>The phrase “should naturally be canceled” in paragraph one above is the usual
                    polite Chinese way of saying “we are canceling it.” In other words in Chinese
                    mentality the whole matter is terminated.</p>
                <p>Nevertheless last sentence of Ministry’s note if literally construed indicates
                    that cancelation is not complete without American action and furnishes logical
                    ground for continuing negotiations.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Schurman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
